  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Transportation obligated $13.3B in contracts for FY2026 to date. The department recorded 14,314 awards, with an average award size of $932K, and obligations are up 47.9% year over year. Annual obligations show a multi-year rise from FY21 through FY25 followed by a larger jump in FY26: FY21 was $7B, FY22 was $8B, FY23 was $8.7B, FY24 was $8.6B, FY25 was $9B, and FY26 is $13.3B. Engineering Services accounted for the largest NAICS category at $1.8B, followed by Telephone Apparatus Manufacturing and Telecommunications Resellers at $1.2B each. HARRIS CORPORATION is the largest vendor at $1.2B, with RAYTHEON COMPANY at $677M and ROHDE & SCHWARZ USA, INC. at $651M rounding out the top three. The portfolio spans construction, systems design, maritime freight, and professional services, showing a mix of infrastructure and technical acquisitions concentrated among a small set of high-dollar vendors and categories.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did Department of Transportation contract spending change over recent years?",
        "body": "The Department of Transportation obligated $13.3B in FY26 to date, up 47.9% year over year. Annual obligations rose from $7B in FY21 to $9B in FY25, then increased to $13.3B in FY26. The number of awards for FY26 to date is 14,314 with an average award size of $932K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the largest spending categories and who wins the biggest contracts?",
        "body": "Engineering Services is the largest NAICS category at $1.8B, followed by Telephone Apparatus Manufacturing and Telecommunications Resellers at $1.2B each. Deep Sea Freight Transportation received $986M and Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction received $787M. HARRIS CORPORATION is the top vendor at $1.2B, followed by RAYTHEON COMPANY at $677M and ROHDE & SCHWARZ USA, INC. at $651M."
      }
    ]
